  "summary_text": "Kyle, yesterday was Labor Day\u2014revenue comparisons are structurally depressed\u2014but Port Washington delivered a signal worth attention, while Hicksville needs immediate focus.\n\nPort Washington ran 8 groomers and generated $501 per groomer, 6% above your 30-day baseline. This is the bright spot: the team executed at elevated productivity despite a holiday-weakened booking day. Core grooming revenue jumped 49% week-over-week, add-on attach improved from 48% to 62%, and transactions climbed 15%. Yesterday performed exactly as it should have given the calendar. Month-to-date, after DOW adjustment, you're tracking 14.7% below expectation\u2014real underperformance, not holiday noise\u2014driven by a 24% drop in appointments. The issue is capacity: you've scheduled 4.1 groomers per day MTD versus 4.9 last month. That's structural. Revenue per groomer is actually up ($495 vs $473), so the team is hunting. You need bodies.\n\nHicksville is the concern. Yesterday's 2 groomers produced $360 per groomer, 10% below baseline. Add-on attach collapsed from 33% to 12%\u2014upsell discipline evaporated. Week-to-date appointments are down 60% and grooming revenue down 39%. Unlike Port Washington, this isn't a capacity story; it's execution. Same DOW-adjusted miss (-14.8%) as Port Washington, but Hicksville has no margin for error at 2.2 groomers per day. You also have zero bather hours scheduled this week and next\u2014that's a gap against Port Washington's 15-hour daily coverage.\n\nToday: Port Washington 7 groomers + 15 bather hours; Hicksville 4 groomers + 0 bather hours. Next 7 days: Port Washington 40 groomer-days, Hicksville 22\u2014a 1.8x capacity delta that won't close your performance gap alone."
